{0 wgpu — OCaml bindings to WebGPU through wgpu-native}

OCaml bindings to the WebGPU C API ([webgpu.h]) and the wgpu-native
extensions ([wgpu.h]), generated from the vendored upstream headers of one
pinned {{:https://github.com/gfx-rs/wgpu-native}wgpu-native} release and bound
with {{:https://github.com/yallop/ocaml-ctypes}ctypes-foreign}.
[libwgpu_native] is opened with [dlopen] on first use: no C stubs are
compiled, no headers are needed at build time, and there is nothing to link.

These are {b raw bindings}. The [wgpu] library mirrors the headers and adds
nothing on top of them; the handful of helpers that no program can avoid
writing live in the separate {!Wgpu_utils} library ([wgpu.utils]).

{b This project was written entirely by AI.} The generator, the runtime, the
generated bindings, the tests, the examples, the CI workflows and this page
were produced by AI agents with no hand-written human code; the only files
that are not AI-authored are the vendored upstream headers under [vendor/].
Correctness rests on automated checks — a C ABI probe compiled from the
vendored headers, an independent header rescan, and real headless GPU runs —
not on human review. See
{{:https://lloyd-g-w.github.io/wgpu-ocaml/#authorship}Authorship} for the
caveats, and {{:https://lloyd-g-w.github.io/wgpu-ocaml/#licensing}Licensing}:
no licence has been chosen for the original code yet.

{1 Where to start}

- {{:https://lloyd-g-w.github.io/wgpu-ocaml/guide.html}The guide} — an
  example-driven walkthrough: installing the native library, handles, enums
  and bit sets, struct defaults, descriptors and lifetimes, compute, offscreen
  rendering, callbacks and polling, errors.
- {{:https://lloyd-g-w.github.io/wgpu-ocaml/guide.html#rendering-to-a-window-with-tsdl}Rendering
  to a window with tsdl} — building a [WGPUSurface] from an SDL2 window
  ([SDL_GetWindowWMInfo] bound with ctypes-foreign), surface capabilities and
  configuration, and the frame loop; the runnable program is
  [examples/sdl_window.with_tsdl.ml].
- {{:https://lloyd-g-w.github.io/wgpu-ocaml/}Project home} — install,
  quick start, status, limitations.
- {{:https://lloyd-g-w.github.io/wgpu-ocaml/design.html}Design contract} —
  the rules the generator, the runtime and the tests must keep.
- {!Wgpu} — the API reference below.

{1 Layout of the [Wgpu] module}

- {b The bindings}, generated from the headers:
  {!Wgpu.Types} (enums, bit sets, opaque handles, structs, callback types) and
  {!Wgpu.Fn} (one OCaml function per C entry point, under its exact C name,
  e.g. {!Wgpu.Fn.wgpuDeviceCreateBuffer}). Struct fields keep their C
  spelling, so the headers stay greppable from OCaml, and every struct module
  has an [init ()] that applies the header's [WGPU_*_INIT] defaults.
- {b Runtime plumbing}: {!Wgpu.Loader} (search path, [dlopen], version gate),
  {!Wgpu.Pin} (the pinned upstream release and its checksums) and
  {!Wgpu.Abi} (the layout dump used by the ABI test).
- {b {!Wgpu.Callback}} — the only correct way to obtain the
  [Ctypes.static_funptr] a WebGPU descriptor wants. ctypes frees the libffi
  closure behind a [Foreign.funptr] when the OCaml value owning it is
  collected, so an unrooted pointer stored in a descriptor is a
  use-after-free; the closures it builds also register the calling thread,
  which is what makes callbacks arriving on wgpu-native's own threads safe.
  This is a correctness requirement, not a convenience.

{1 [wgpu.utils]}

{!Wgpu_utils} is a separate library with a deliberately narrow admission rule:
a helper belongs there only if nearly every program has to write it anyway and
the other wgpu-native bindings ship it too. That is three things —
{!Wgpu_utils.Sync} (the synchronous adapter and device requests),
{!Wgpu_utils.Buffer} (a blocking buffer read driven by [wgpuDevicePoll]) and
{!Wgpu_utils.String_view} ([WGPUStringView] conversion). Failures are
[result] values. Nothing else: no descriptor builders, no error sink, no
logging wrapper.

{1 A taste}

{[
module T = Wgpu.Types
module F = Wgpu.Fn

let () =
  let instance = F.wgpuCreateInstance (Ctypes.from_voidp T.InstanceDescriptor.t Ctypes.null) in
  let adapter = Result.get_ok (Wgpu_utils.Sync.request_adapter instance) in
  let device =
    Result.get_ok
      (Wgpu_utils.Sync.request_device adapter)
  in
  let queue = F.wgpuDeviceGetQueue device in

  (* Descriptors always start from the header's INIT defaults. *)
  let desc = T.BufferDescriptor.init () in
  Ctypes.setf desc T.BufferDescriptor.usage
    (* copy_dst is what makes wgpuQueueWriteBuffer legal *)
    T.BufferUsage.(combine [ storage; copy_dst; copy_src ]);
  Ctypes.setf desc T.BufferDescriptor.size (Unsigned.UInt64.of_int 1024);
  let buffer = F.wgpuDeviceCreateBuffer device (Ctypes.addr desc) in

  F.wgpuBufferRelease buffer;
  F.wgpuQueueRelease queue;
  F.wgpuDeviceRelease device;
  F.wgpuAdapterRelease adapter;
  F.wgpuInstanceRelease instance
]}

{1 Things worth knowing before you start}

- {b Start every struct from [init ()]}, never from [Ctypes.make]: zero is not
  a valid default in WebGPU (undefined limits are [0xFFFFFFFF], a depth clear
  value is [NaN], an extension struct's [sType] must be set).
- {b Keep what a descriptor points at alive} until the call has returned. A
  [WGPUStringView], an array of entries or a chained struct is a bare pointer
  into memory ctypes owns, and the GC frees it as soon as the OCaml value
  holding it dies.
- {b Lifetimes are yours.} No finaliser releases a GPU handle for you.
- {b Errors are asynchronous.} Install an uncaptured-error callback in the
  [WGPUDeviceDescriptor] you pass to
  {!Wgpu_utils.Sync.request_device}, or push an error scope; a raw entry point
  tells you nothing but a possibly-null handle.
- {b One domain at a time.} Callbacks may arrive on threads wgpu-native owns,
  so entry points release the OCaml domain lock and closures register the
  calling thread and re-take it; what is not supported is driving wgpu from
  several {e domains}, since registered foreign threads attach to domain 0.
- {b Futures do not work in this release.} [WGPUFuture] and
  [wgpuInstanceWaitAny] are not implemented in wgpu-native v29; asynchronous
  work is driven by [wgpuDevicePoll] and [wgpuInstanceProcessEvents] instead.
- {b Some bound entry points abort the process} because wgpu-native leaves
  them [unimplemented!()]. They are listed in {!Wgpu.Pin.unimplemented}.
